<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Daowu of Northern Wei</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">371–409</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Tuoba Gui</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba">Tuoba</a> Gui, posthumously Emperor Daowu, was the founder of the Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei">Wei dynasty</a>. A grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba-shiyijian">Tuoba Shiyijian</a>, he restored the Tuoba state of Dai in 386 after the collapse of Former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> and soon renamed it Wei. He defeated Later Yan at Canhe Slope in 395, took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china-plain">North China Plain</a> and in 398 made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datong">Pingcheng</a> his capital and proclaimed himself emperor. He was murdered by his son in 409.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-daowu-of-northern-wei.jpg" alt="Emperor Daowu of Northern Wei" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TAOZIlovewiki · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E4%BA%91%E5%86%88%E7%9F%B3%E7%AA%9F03--CNSX-352-001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
